<!--ID=5B95B1CC-2C7B-494F-B746-CF22A0E779B7;Version=1|{"Locales":{"en-US":{"AutoApplyCustomLexiconFiles":[{}]},"en-GB":{"AutoApplyCustomLexiconFiles":[{}]}}}-->
<speak xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2001/10/synthesis"
       xmlns:mstts="http://www.w3.org/2001/mstts"
       xmlns:emo="http://www.w3.org/2009/10/emotionml"
       version="1.0"
       xml:lang="en-GB">
  <voice name="en-GB-OllieMultilingualNeural">
    <prosody rate="-11.50%" pitch="-3.00%">
            Jesus continues by saying that people do not light a lamp and put it under a basket, but on a stand, and it gives light to all in the house.<break time="500ms"/>
      The image is domestic, ordinary, and clear: light is for illumination, not concealment.<break time="500ms"/>
      Matthew's picture of light moves from star, to dawn, to the lamp set high enough to serve the room around it.<break time="1000ms"/>
      The disciple is not told to create the flame, but not to hide what God has given.<break time="500ms"/>
      A lamp on a stand is useful, steady, and quiet, doing its work without noise.<break time="500ms"/>
      This gentle household image keeps the idea of light from becoming proud, because its purpose remains service.<break time="1800ms"/>
      The Lord continues to care after the crisis.<break time="1000ms"/>
      A lamp is placed where its light can serve the house.<break time="1000ms"/>
      The light Jesus gives is meant for faithful visibility and useful witness.<break time="1000ms"/>
      You do not need dramatic display to shine faithfully.<break time="1800ms"/>
      You can rest.<break time="3800ms"/>
    </prosody>
  </voice>
</speak>